@@ -0,0 +1,328 @@
+Beat my track vehicle audio bank v1 — 2026-10-06
+
+Continuity correction: rebuilt with float64 resampling indexes to remove silent
+gaps introduced by Windows int32 overflow. Updated derivative hashes: manifest.json.
+
+Recorded engine, rolling and body source masters:
+Nox Sound Design, Essentials Series, CC0 1.0.
+https://nox-sound-design.itch.io/essentials-series-sfx-nox-sound
+
+Additional recorded engine source masters:
+Joseph SARDIN, “AC Cobra Engine”, BigSoundBank, CC0 1.0.
+https://bigsoundbank.com/ac-cobra-engine-s0534.html
+DenisChardonnet, “Motor car #1”, BigSoundBank, CC0 1.0.
+https://bigsoundbank.com/motor-car-1-s0290.html
+DenisChardonnet, “Motorcycle”, BigSoundBank, CC0 1.0.
+https://bigsoundbank.com/motorcycle-s0281.html
+
+Recorded tire squeal:
+“Car tire squeal skid loop” by audible-edge (Tom Haigh), edited into a loop
+by qubodup, licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 3.0 Unported.
+https://opengameart.org/content/car-tire-squeal-skid-loop
+https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/
+Changes: mono 44.1 kHz resampling, filtering, loop crossfade, loudness mastering
+and 16-bit dither. Attribution applies to tire.wav and audio/video containing it.
+
+All recorded masters are edited for fictional vehicles. No manufacturer
+affiliation or claim of exact engine reproduction. Engine variants use cuts,
+transposition, equalization, on-load/coast filtering and crossfaded loops.
+Surface/impact revision 2 (2026-10-08) replaces the door/trunk impact and
+footstep-derived stone derivatives. New source recordings/sound designs:
+"Collision" by qubodup, CC0 1.0.
+https://freesound.org/people/qubodup/sounds/332058/
+This is a designed mechanical collision based on a CC0 drone recording,
+not a field recording of a crashing car. Filtered/transposed/edited into
+light, medium and heavy hits, with Nox body movement as a settling layer.
+"gravel drive.wav" by cognito perceptu, CC0 1.0.
+https://freesound.org/people/cognito%20perceptu/sounds/97605/
+Recorded car-on-gravel driving supplies separate granular and low rolling
+beds; the spray blends a small amount of Nox dirty-ground vehicle foley.
+"Tapping things with stones.wav" by KALeigh0218, CC0 1.0.
+https://freesound.org/people/KALeigh0218/sounds/655924/
+Short damped stone contacts, filtered/cut/mastered as underside taps.
+These three sources use the publicly provided HQ MP3 previews, decoded
+offline with the pinned Godot build. They are not lossless source masters.
+The old Nox pebble/door source records remain in the provenance ledger,
+but are no longer the active impact/grit derivatives. Grass uses a longer
+recorded grass-driving segment with fuller low/mid rolling texture.
+Timing revision 3 (2026-10-08) front-loads the existing Nox body-movement
+transient for chassis/landing/kerb/shift events. Its strongest attack now
+occurs near 4 ms instead of near 104 ms. All other 44 clip hashes remain
+unchanged from surface/impact revision 2; no additional source is introduced.
+Continuous-desert revision 4 (2026-10-08) adds sand_debris.wav: a 9.9-second
+granular noise texture built offline from the existing KALeigh0218 CC0 stone
+contacts and cognito perceptu CC0 gravel-driving recording. Short damped grains
+overlap densely with irregular spacing, pitch and amplitude; the long envelope
+is leveled and loop-crossfaded. All prior 45 clip hashes remain unchanged.
+Desert stops scheduling the separate grit clips and mixes the new continuous
+texture with its rolling bed and quieter sand spray. This is designed recorded
+foley, not a dedicated under-car field recording or runtime synthesis.
+Tone/balance revision 5 follows the owner's rejection of the bright first
+continuous candidate. Recorded granular driving now dominates (75%) with
+subdued stone-grain detail (25%), a softer 4.4 kHz low-pass and leveled full-bed
+envelope. Speed shifts pitch slightly; all desert ground layers are quieter
+than the engine, with intensity changes for power/slip. Prior 45 masters remain
+exact; the rejected candidate and actual mix are preserved in task evidence.
+Collision-direct revision 6 (2026-10-08) trims only impact_0/1/2 to their
+strongest starting transient and removes the collision-only 3 ms fade-in.
+Their first PCM frame is now the peak, with no quiet lead-in. Original
+qubodup/Nox source attribution remains; other 43 clip hashes are unchanged.
+Wind and water noise beds are original offline sound design.
+
+Wet asphalt correction, 2026-10-09: wet_road.wav blends filtered Nox CC0
+car-interior rolling with original sustained spray hiss. Mono resampling,
+filtering, crossfaded looping, loudness mastering and dither. This is a designed
+wet-tire derivative, not a dedicated wet-road field recording. The road voice
+follows speed and four-wheel asphalt contact; no continuous storm rain audio.
+Source/derivative filenames and SHA-256 values: manifest.json.
+All sources are local build inputs. The game makes no audio-generation/API calls.
+
+Compact engine revision 1, 2026-10-10: only compact_0/1/2_load/coast.wav are
+revoiced from the existing Nox CC0 1000/2000 RPM rear-exterior recordings.
+Measured repeating engine orders are transposed to 40/100/200 Hz at the
+1200/3000/6000 playback anchors, with warmer low/mid equalization, restrained
+high-frequency detail and quieter coast variants. The front-exterior high
+layer is replaced by the matching rear-exterior perspective. Offline filtering,
+loop crossfades, headroom mastering and deterministic dither remain. No new
+recording, licence or exact-engine reproduction claim is introduced.
+
+Compact loop revision 2, 2026-10-10: the same six engine derivatives now
+filter the complete recordings before cutting, align head/tail waveform phase
+within two engine periods, and apply final filtering to the completed periodic
+loop. Mid/high banks use longer source sections. This reduces recurring fade
+modulation and cut-edge ringing; no new source, runtime voice or gain is added.
+
+Compact sustain revision 3, 2026-10-10: compact_1/2_load/coast.wav now use
+phase-aligned averages of the same recorded Nox firing cycles, sustained at
+exact 100/200 Hz for eight seconds. A restrained original offline noise bed
+uses the broad spectral shape of the recording's residual; it carries no
+wandering source tones. Coast is filtered from the same complete sustain.
+This authored recorded/synthetic derivative removes the previous short
+recording's repeating pitch drift. The low/idle pair and other 41 WAVs are
+unchanged. No runtime synthesis, source purchase or new third-party licence.
+
+Complete loop revision 8, 2026-10-10: all five engine families now sustain
+averaged recorded firing cycles at coherent neighboring RPM orders, with a
+restrained original offline mechanical/air noise bed. Eight-second whole-cycle
+PCM retains each cleared source's pulse colour without replaying rev/stop
+transients. The compact's previous mid/high sustains remain byte-identical;
+its idle pair is sustained in the same way. Other engine timbres are revoiced
+and require listening acceptance; no exact real-engine reproduction is claimed.
+Surface/wind/water derivatives use correlation-aware power-preserving joins
+and circular envelope control. Road, wind and gravel sections are longer.
+The tire's pinned zero endpoints now lie at a natural quiet crossing, replacing
+the short repeated fade-to-silence; audible-edge/qubodup CC BY 3.0 attribution
+and the modification notice above still apply. All one-shot PCM remains exact.
+Runtime loops supply one cyclic interpolation frame beyond the loop endpoint
+to prevent fractional-pitch interpolation into resource silence padding.
+
+Directional collision revision 1, 2026-10-10: scrape.wav blends the existing
+audible-edge (Tom Haigh)/qubodup tire squeal (CC BY 3.0) with dense offline
+transposed stone/metal grains from KALeigh0218 and granular driving from cognito
+perceptu (both CC0). Filtering, envelope control, cyclic crossfade, loudness
+mastering and dither produce a sustained barrier screech. The tire attribution
+and modification notice above also apply to scrape.wav and previews using it.
+impact_0/1/2.wav revoice the same qubodup CC0 collision with deeper Nox CC0 body
+movement and longer medium/heavy settling tails, retaining immediate attacks.
+These are designed fictional vehicle impacts, not dedicated fence/bridge/crash
+field recordings. Source and derivative checksums remain in manifest.json.
+
+Metallic scrape revision 2, 2026-10-10: scrape.wav now emphasizes KALeigh0218
+CC0 stone/metal resonant tails, cut into longer, closely transposed overlapping
+grains (nominal mix weight 76%), with Nox CC0 body-panel vibration (16%) and
+restrained audible-edge (Tom Haigh)/qubodup CC BY 3.0 tire strain (8%). The
+prior gravel layer is removed. Offline filters, envelope mastering, crossfaded
+looping and dither retain sustained friction and similar loudness. Designed
+body-on-barrier foley, not a dedicated fence scrape field recording. Tire
+attribution/modification notice still applies; source hashes: manifest.json.
+
+Owner-selected crash revision 3, 2026-10-10: impact_0/1/2.wav use the owner's
+supplied u_mgq59j5ayf-sound-effect-car-crash-394903.mp3. Creator and third-party
+license were not supplied; owner selected/authorized this recording for local
+game use. Do not relabel it as CC0 or as an original studio recording. The
+source remains unchanged in local build inputs. Mono pinned-engine decode,
+quiet lead-in/main-attack trim, restrained filtering, peak/loudness mastering,
+shorter light/medium cuts and tail fade; heavy retains original pitch and tail.
+No former qubodup collision or Nox settling layer remains in these three clips.
+Exact encoded/decoded/derivative SHA-256 values are in manifest.json. Metallic
+scraping and the other 45 bank WAVs remain unchanged by this revision.
+
+Irregular low scrape revision 3, 2026-10-10: scrape.wav replaces the rejected
+dense chatter with slower, lower-pitched recorded metal tails (nominal 60%),
+body-panel rubbing (35%) and restrained tire strain (5%). Metal pitch0.46-0.68,
+uneven90-310ms stroke spacing, smooth variable pressure and2.4kHz low-pass.
+The longer11.5s loop retains its authored intensity changes rather than the
+previous100ms envelope flattening. Same KALeigh0218/Nox CC0 and audible-edge/
+qubodup CC BY3.0 sources/attribution; no new recording or license. The owner's
+crash derivatives are unchanged. This remains designed friction foley.
+
+Continuous body-scrape revision 4, 2026-10-11: scrape.wav now uses only
+"metal soft scrape.wav" by taure, CC0 1.0:
+https://freesound.org/people/taure/sounds/341280/
+The author records rubbing a marker over a cookie-tin lid. This is repurposed
+recorded metal friction, not a field recording of a car scraping a fence.
+Public HQ MP3 preview, pinned-engine mono decode; remove strikes/start-stops,
+select sustained rubbing tails, pitch0.65/filter85-1300Hz, power-corrected180ms
+joins, restrained slow variation, headroom/loop mastering and16-bit dither.
+Old struck-metal grains, body thuds and tire strain are absent from scrape.wav.
+The eleven-and-a-half-second loop and the owner's crash clips are retained.
+License page, encoded source and float master stay in non-exported build inputs;
+source/derivative SHA-256 and decoder data: manifest.json.
+
+Owner-selected rail scrape revision 5, 2026-10-11: scrape.wav replaces the
+previous rubbing derivative with "S023_Metal_Rail_Scrape_Mono.wav" by Pól,
+Creative Commons Zero 1.0:
+https://freesound.org/people/P%C3%B3l/sounds/385939/
+The author describes a rally car scraping along a metal rail barrier.
+Public HQ MP3 preview decoded locally by the pinned engine. Keep0.35-3.65s,
+exclude contact/settling ends,0.5s cyclic power crossfade, common headroom/DC
+removal/dither. Original pitch and spectrum retained; no added layers or EQ.
+Loop2.8s; all47 other bank WAVs, including the owner's crash, remain exact.
+Encoded/decoded/derivative hashes and source/decoder metadata: manifest.json.
+
+Grass onset/kerb revision 1, 2026-10-11: grass.wav is unchanged; live wheel
+contact now starts it promptly with stronger partial-contact mixing. The new
+kerb_roll.wav blends the existing cognito perceptu CC0 gravel-driving rumble
+with damped dense Nox CC0 body foley, filtered70-1600Hz, cyclic joins,
+envelope/loudness mastering and16-bit dither. Speed controls rolling rate.
+Crossings reuse the existing chassis.wav on actual raised-kerb contact. This
+is authored curb tyre/body feedback, not a dedicated curb field recording.
+All48 prior WAVs, including the owner's crash and Pól's rail scrape, remain
+byte-identical. Credits/source URLs above and exact hashes in manifest.json.
+
+Kerb revision 2, 2026-10-11: kerb_roll.wav replaces the gravel/body-tap blend
+with a derivative of "TRANSPORTATION CAR PASS BY 01.wav" by sengjinn,
+Creative Commons Zero 1.0:
+https://freesound.org/people/sengjinn/sounds/176215/
+The source is a car passing over rumble strips/sleeper lines, rather than a
+dedicated circuit-kerb recording. Public HQ MP3 preview, pinned-engine mono
+decode, close-pass excerpt6.6-9.4s,80-1750Hz EQ with low-mid emphasis,
+circular power crossfade, envelope/loudness mastering and16-bit dither.
+No added body-knock train or gravel layer. Runtime pitch limited0.65-1.35;
+entry uses the existing quieter/lower chassis cue. All48 other bank WAVs
+remain exact. License page and source are offline build inputs; source and
+derivative hashes/decoder metadata are in manifest.json.
+
+Clean kerb revision 3, 2026-10-11: kerb_roll.wav is an original offline design
+with no recorded source. Damped broad rubber/body resonances and restrained
+concrete rolling texture are excited by deterministic generated noise; shallow
+24Hz flutter with smooth phase/pressure variation,70-1400Hz final EQ, cyclic
+power join, loudness mastering and16-bit dither. No engine, traffic, microphone
+background or field recording is used in this asset. Previous kerb recording
+credits above are historical only. Generator/seed/derivative hash are in the
+bank manifest; all48 other bank WAVs remain exact. Runtime detection, existing
+quiet entry cue and bounded speed pitch remain unchanged.
+
+Slower kerb pulse revision 4, 2026-10-11: the original clean kerb design now
+uses an8Hz repeating flutter instead of24Hz, with the same underlying rubber
+rumble, continuous texture, shallow depth and smooth variation. Runtime speed
+pitch maps this to roughly5.2-10.8 pulses per second. Generator/provenance and
+native pulse rate are in manifest.json. No field recording is used; all48
+other WAVs and runtime contact/mix behavior remain unchanged.
+
+Kerb thuds revision 5, 2026-10-11: original offline synthesis replaces the
+flutter texture with six soft rubber/body thuds per second at native pitch.
+Rounded2.5ms attacks, briefly falling150-to105Hz resonance, low-passed contact
+detail and damped27ms tails; a quiet low bed joins the tails. Small pitch/gain
+variation; cyclic power join,65-1100Hz final EQ and16-bit dither. Individual
+thud envelopes are preserved instead of100ms texture leveling. No field
+recording. Generator/seed/pulse rate/derivative hash are in manifest.json;
+all48 other bank WAVs and runtime contact/mix/entry behavior remain exact.
+
+Offroad texture revision 1, 2026-10-11: grass.wav and loose.wav replace their
+bright leveled recording beds with original offline tyre/soil design. Low
+100-650Hz rolling with smooth uneven pressure, irregular overlapping soft
+rubber loading and restrained short crunch/rustle grains. Grass uses a softer
+75-1500Hz final band; gravel retains more crunch up to2300Hz. No field ambience
+or fixed repeating kerb pulse. Natural variation is retained rather than100ms
+texture leveling. Cyclic joins, common headroom and16-bit dither remain.
+Generator/seed/derivative hashes are in manifest.json; former source credits
+are historical for these two clips. All47 other bank WAVs remain exact,
+including accepted kerb thuds, crashes, rail scrape and desert textures.
+
+Offroad repair revision 2, 2026-10-11: grass.wav now uses the existing Nox
+"Vehicle_Truck_Drive_Grass_Exterior_Loop_Stereo.wav" CC0 source and loose.wav
+uses cognito perceptu's CC0 gravel_drive_cc0.wav, both credited above. Remove
+the rejected synthesized bass/body notes. Recorded rolling/rustle retain
+their natural envelope; overlapped spectral attenuation restrains narrow
+motor harmonics. Grass260-1900Hz/gravel180-2600Hz EQ, lower level, cyclic
+join and16-bit dither. No generated ground tones. Source and derivative hashes
+are in manifest.json; all47 other WAVs, including every engine bank and the
+accepted kerb thuds, remain exact. Engine sound provenance is unchanged.
+
+Owner grass rustle revision 1, 2026-10-11: grass.wav derives solely from the
+owner-supplied dragon-studio-grass-rustling-05-511299.mp3 and
+dragon-studio-grass-rustling-04-511301.mp3. Dragon Studio attribution is inferred
+from filenames; source-site/license documentation was not supplied or verified.
+Owner-authorized local use; retain this provenance rather than claim a CC0 grant.
+Pinned-engine mono decode, active-section excerpts, alternating variable cuts
+with120ms soft overlaps/200-300ms advance, slow500ms energy control,
+140-4200Hz EQ, cyclic power join/headroom/16-bit dither. No generated noise or
+body tone is added. Loop11.5s; runtime rustle pace0.70-1.35 with travel speed,
+existing immediate contact/intensity/silence rules. Source/decoder/derivative
+hashes are in manifest.json. All48 other bank WAVs, accepted kerb thuds and
+the Engine-sidechain mixer repair are retained.
+
+Snowbank audio revision 1, 2026-10-22: snow.wav and snow_hit.wav derive only
+from Nox Sound Design's Footsteps_Snow_Run_01/03, Footsteps_Snow_Hard_Run_02
+and Footsteps_Snow_Jump_Land_01 recordings in Essentials Series (CC0-1.0).
+Source: https://nox-sound-design.itch.io/essentials-series-sfx-nox-sound
+The author's CC0 statement and personal/commercial permission were verified
+on 2026-10-22. Snow compression recordings are edited as an irregular dense
+overlap without walking cadence, EQ/level/headroom mastered and cyclically
+joined; the separate short compression attack marks bank entry. No generated
+noise or tones are added. These are authored snow foley, not recordings of a
+car hitting snow. Source/archive identities and derivative hashes are retained
+in snow_manifest.json. Existing vehicle masters remain unchanged.
+
+Kerb quality revision 6, 2026-10-22: kerb_roll.wav and its Ogg derivative are
+original offline project-authored dry tyre contacts and damped body chatter.
+Broad generated rubber excitation, fixed short body modes and restrained
+upper-mid attacks replace the falling sine notes. Six contacts/second at native
+pitch remain; no external recording is used. Seed2026102261 and reproduction
+metadata are in manifest.json. Existing NumPy/FFmpeg are offline tools only.
+The master (-18.51 LUFS) and actual Ogg (-18.47 LUFS) closely match the prior
+master (-18.50 LUFS). See docs/KERB_SOUND_QUALITY_2026_10_22.md.
+
+Snowbank audio revision 2, 2026-10-22: same cleared snow sources; snow.wav now
+uses only the two soft-snow recordings in dense35-70ms overlaps,150ms cyclic
+energy control and85-3000Hz shaping. snow_hit.wav uses the recorded landing
+slowed for body weight, trimmed to the compression attack and85-2600Hz shaped;
+its half-peak attack arrives within0.6ms. No generated noise/tones or other
+vehicle masters are changed. Updated source/derivative identities and exact
+processing are in snow_manifest.json and scripts/build_snow_audio.py.
+
+Cobblestone kerb revision 7, 2026-10-23: kerb_roll.wav/.ogg derive from
+Car rolling on cobblestone.wav by orlandorizo, CC0, recorded inside a small
+compact on bumpy cobblestones. Source: https://freesound.org/people/orlandorizo/sounds/591078/
+The public HQ MP3 preview, rather than original 48kHz/24-bit WAV, is decoded
+to mono44.1kHz PCM. Cut8-16s, broad100-6500Hz shaping, cyclic join and fixed
+master gain retain natural uneven contacts. No generated tones/noise or fixed
+pulse train. Source hashes/cut are in manifest.json; runtime Ogg has the matching
+compression receipt. Source is a cobblestone character reference, not claimed
+track-kerb field audio. Details: docs/KERB_COBBLESTONE_2026_10_23.md.
+
+Cobblestone/thud kerb revision 8, 2026-10-23: retains the above cobblestone
+excerpt and adds a quiet six-per-second recorded body pulse underneath it.
+Body source: Vehicle_Truck_Body_Movement_Interior_Mono_01.wav, Nox Sound Design,
+Essentials Series, CC0-1.0:
+https://nox-sound-design.itch.io/essentials-series-sfx-nox-sound
+Source cut0.92-1.045s,95-420Hz shaping,4ms attack/36ms decay/12ms release,
+small level variations; body-layer RMS is0.38 of cobblestone before mastering.
+The original uneven cobblestone timing remains; the added body cadence is
+authored. No generated tone/noise is added. Fixed master gain matches revision7.
+Both source hashes and processing are retained in manifest.json. Details:
+docs/KERB_SUBTLE_THUD_2026_10_23.md. This supersedes revision7's no-pulse design.
+
+Original snow entry revision 3, 2026-10-23: snow_hit.wav is the packed-snow
+composite of the existing CC0 Nox Sound Design snow landing and hard-snow run.
+Independently peak-aligned recorded compression, 50-580Hz slowed recorded body
+and snow detail use a short release and rounded density control. The first
+100ms of the mastered entry is about2.3dB stronger than revision2; strong onset
+is0.204ms. Alternative powder and heavy-bank auditions use the same cleared
+snow sources. No Soundsnap recording or other reference audio is included;
+the site's previews were not usable here and no listening comparison with them
+is claimed. Continuous snow.wav remains byte-identical to revision2. Exact
+source/derivative hashes and processing are in snow_manifest.json and
+scripts/build_snow_audio.py. Auditions, checks and acceptance limits:
+docs/ORIGINAL_SNOW_IMPACT_2026_10_23.md.
